    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0425" n="375" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg375"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Dun to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tokyo, Japan</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-10-23"><hi rendition="#italic">October 23, 1894</hi></date>.
                        (Received November 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 181.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your instruction of the 22d ultimo relative to the two Japanese boys under
                    arrest at Shanghai charged as spies.</p>
                <pb facs="0426" n="376" xml:id="pg376"/>
                <p>In connection with the last paragraph of your instruction, in which you inform me
                    that the Chinese minister at Washington “agreed that the two alleged Japanese
                    spies should not be tried until Colonel Denby returned to China,” I regret to
                    say that the young men in question were executed at Nankin on the 8th instant,
                    before the colonel had reached Shanghai.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edwin Dun.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
